          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Broadly speaking,
            %em quantum logic
            is meant to be a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            that is to traditional formal logic as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            is to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            %em express
            the statements whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            is the totality of all what is verifiable by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurement"} measurement
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"}> quantum measurement
            ).
          %p
            In its traditional and default meaning due to (
            %a{:href => "#BirkhoffvonNeumann36"}> Birkhoff-vonNeumann 1936
            ) “quantum logic” refers specifically to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthocomplemented+lattice"} orthocomplemented lattice
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspaces"} linear subspaces
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} of quantum states
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Yetter90"} Yetter 90
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Pratt92"} Pratt 92
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Pratt93"} 93
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AbramskyDuncan05"} Abramsky-Duncan 05
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Girard11"} Girard 11
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            (
            %a{:href => "#Girard"}> Girard 87
            ), the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
          %p
            There is also the proposal (
            %a{:href => "#HeunenLandsmanSpitters07"}> Heunen-Landsman-Spitters 07
            ) that quantum logic should be understood as being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+toposes"} Bohr toposes
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
            the quantum analog of classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic+gates"} logic gates
            are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic+gates"} quantum logic gates
          %h2#History History and Critique
          %p
            Typically in the literature the term “quantum logic” is taken to refer very specifically to the first proposal for such a formalization due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BirkhoffvonNeumann36"} Birkhoff-von Neumann 1936
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+states"} of states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            about the system are taken to correspond to (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projections"} projections
            to)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspaces"} closed subspaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
            given by inclusion of such subspaces. Hence Birkhoff-von Neumann quantum logic is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspaces"} linear subspaces
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            (regarded as a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+lattice"} Hilbert lattice
          %p
            This formalization is often understood as being the default meaning of “quantum logic”. But the proposal has later been much criticised, for its lack of key properties that one would demand of any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
          %p#NonLogic
            For instance in
            %a{:href => "#AbramskyCoecke2007"} Abramsky &amp; Coecke (2007)
            it is called a “non-logic”:
          %blockquote
            %p#NonLogic
              The term quantum logic is usually understood in connection with the 1936 Birkhoff-von Neumann proposal to consider the (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"}> closed
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspaces"} linear subspaces
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
              ordered by inclusion as the formal expression of the logical distinction between quantum and classical physics. While in classical logic we have deduction, the linear subspaces of a Hilbert space form a non-distributive lattice and hence there is no obvious notion of implication or deduction. Quantum logic was therefore always seen as logically very weak, or even as a non-logic. In addition, it has never given a satisfactory account of compound systems and entanglement.
          %p
            Here by “no
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deduction"} deduction
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deduction+theorem"} deduction theorem
          %p
            And for example in (
            %a{:href => "#HeunenLandsmanSpitters07"}> Heunen-Landsman-Spitters 07, p. 4
            ) it says the following:
          %blockquote
            %p Attractive and revolutionary as this spatial quantum “logic” may appear it faces severe problems. The main logical drawbacks are:
            %ol
              %li
                %p Due to its lack of distributivity, quantum ‘logic’ is difficult to interpret as a logical structure.
              %li
                %p In particular, despite various proposals no satisfactory implication operator has been found (so that there is no deductive system in quantum logic).
              %li
                %p
                  Quantum ‘logic’ is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional
                  language; no satisfactory generalization to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
                  has been found.
            %p
              Quantum logic is also problematic from a physical perspective. Since (by various theorems and wide agreement) quantum probabilities do not admit an ignorance interpretation,
              = succeed "-valued" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Born+rule"} Born rule
              cannot be regarded as sharp (i.e. {0, 1}-valued) truth values muddled by human ignorance. This implies that, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = [a \in \Delta]
              represents a quantum-mechanical proposition, it is wrong to say that either
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              or its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
              holds, but we just do not know which of these alternatives applies. However, in quantum logic one has the law of the excluded middle in the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee x^\perp = 1
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            But notice that one may argue that the first three points here are squarely resolved by thinking of BvN-quantum logic as embedded into
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            = succeed "-outcomes" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretations
          %p
            In
            %a{:href => "#Girard11"} Girard 2011, page xii
            it says:
          %blockquote
            %p#MagisteralMistake Among the magisterial mistakes of logic, one will first mention quantum logic, whose ridiculousness can only be ascribed to a feeling of superiority of the language – and ideas, even bad, as soon as they take a written form – over the physical world. Quantum logic is indeed a sort of punishment inflicted on nature, guilty of not yielding to the prejudices of logicians… just like Xerxes had the Hellespont – which had destroyed a boat bridge – whipped.
          %p
            For more and more objective criticism see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Girard11"} Girard 2011, section 17
          %p
            Girard had introduced the class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            systems called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            and it has been argued that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            and more generally
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            does faithfully capture the essence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            (
            %a{:href => "#Yetter90"}> Yetter 90
            \.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Pratt92"} Pratt 92
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AbramskyDuncan05"} Abramsky-Duncan 05
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Duncan06"} Duncan 06
            %a{:href => "#BaezStay09"} Baez-Stay 09
            for an introductory exposition). This is due to the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of linear logic is in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
            such as those used in the description of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} finite quantum mechanics in terms of dagger-compact categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of (finite dimensional)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            whose
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            form the Birkhoff-von Neumann style quantum logic does interpret
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
          %p
            This is stated explicitly for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Pratt92"}> Pratt 92, p.4
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p These objections are overcome in the extension of quantum logic to linear logic as a dynamic quantum logic.
          %p
            Notice that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
            in a category of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"}> finite dimensional
            )
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspaces"} linear subspaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a{:href => "#Crown75"} Crown 1975
            and then later with more of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            in place and emphasizing
            = succeed "-structures" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-category"} dagger
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Heunen08"} Heunen 2008
            %a{:href => "#Harding08"} Harding 2008
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HeunenJacobs09"} Heunen-Jacobs 2009
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#CoeckeHeunenKissinger13"} Coecke-Heunen-Kissinger 2013
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#CCGP09"} CCGP 09, section 9.3
          %blockquote
            %p
              both orthologic (or weakenings thereof) and linear logic share the failure of lattice distributivity. In particular, the fragment of linear logic that includes just
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
              and the additive connectives is nothing but a version of the paraconsistent quantum logic PQL.
          %p
            That seems to make much of the above-listed criticism appear in a different light. For instance there is also a natural notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            and that does yield a well-behaved kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifiers"} quantifiers
            for linear logic.
          %h2#approaches Approaches
          %h3#BirkhoffVonNeumannQuantumLogic Birkhoff-von Neumann quantum logic
          %p
            The original proposal for quantum logic by
            %a{:href => "#BirkhoffvonNeumann36"} Birkhoff &amp; von Neumann 1936
            (BvN quantum logic) is to regard, given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} of states
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+lattice"} Hilbert lattice
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspaces"} linear subspaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi cl
                  %mi.mathscript ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subset_{cl} \mathscr{H}
            as the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            about the quantum system.
          %p Then:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                logical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjunction"} conjunction
                is given by intersection of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspaces"} linear subspaces
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo ∩
                        %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      V_1 \wedge V_2
                      \\;\coloneqq\;
                      V_1 \cap_{\mathscr{H}} V_2
                      \\,,
            %li
              %p
                logical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
                is given by forming the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+span"} linear span
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∨
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi Span
                        %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      V_1 \vee V_2
                      \\;\coloneqq\;
                      Span_{\mathscr{H}}(V_1 \, V_2)
              %p
                of two
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspaces"} linear subspaces
            %li
              %p
                logical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
                is given by forming
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+complement"} orthogonal complement
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi V
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi V
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo ⊥
                            %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\not V \;\coloneqq\; V^{\perp_{\mathscr{H}}}
                      \\,.
          %p
            Notice that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            in the conjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∨
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1 \vee V_2
            may be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+combinations"} linear combinations
            of states in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition+principle"} superposition principle
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributivity"} distribute
            over conjunction, and hence the BvN lattice of quantum propositions is not a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
          %h3#tensor_logic_of_quantales Tensor logic of Quantales
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+Hilbert+spaces"} tensor product of Hilbert spaces
            – which in the jargon of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            would be called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            – makes the
            %a{:href => "#BirkhoffVonNeumannQuantumLogic"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+lattice"} Hilbert lattice
            a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantale"} quantale
          %h3#LinearLogicAndDependentLinearTypeTheory Linear logic and dependent linear type theory
          %p
            We point out that — at least for the
            %a{:href => "Hilbert+space#FiniteDimensionalInnerProductSpaces"} finite dimensional Hilbert spaces
            relevant in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            —
            %a{:href => "#BirkhoffVonNeumannQuantumLogic"} BvN quantum logic
            is just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            of a kind of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type"} linearly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+types"} linear types
            inside
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpreted
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/VectBund"} VectBund
            over the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
          %p
            (Notice that it is the linearity of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a{:href => "linear+logic#AbsenceOfWeakeningAndContraction"} there
            for more) both the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no-cloning+theorem"} no-cloning theorem
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no-deleting+theorem"} no-deleting theorem
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
          %p
            Namely, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+types"} linear types
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/VectBund"} VectBund
            form the subcategory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional vector space
            admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hermitian+inner+product"} hermitian inner product
            making it a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjunction"} conjunction
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
            this Hilbert space structure plays no role and we may and will ignore it.
          %p
            So given
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi FinDimVect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} \in FinDimVect
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            about it in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            is, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-truncation"} (-1)-truncated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+subspace"} linear subspace
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi.mathscript 𝒱
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi FinDimVect
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\mathscr{V} \hookrightarrow \mathscr{H})
                  \\;\in\;
                  \\tau_{-1}\big(FinDimVect_{/\mathscr{H}}\big)
                  \\,.
          %p
            We need to consider the (co)fiber products of such objects (discussed
            %a{:href => "FinDimVect#FiberCoProducts"} here
            at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
          %p
            Logical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjunction"} conjunction
            of such slice objects is given by their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            in the slice, hence by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
            of their inclusion maps in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FinDimVect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinDimVect
          %svg{:height => "124.212pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 141.294 124.212", :width => "141.294pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
            %defs
              %g
                %symbol{:id => "EMLIhDVVCtUZz_ROYYMclDIgSB8=-glyph0-0", :overflow => "visible"}
